; worldencounter.eval — a random encounter is built, fought in, and thrown away.
;
; What it is really watching:
;   - the encounter level exists ONLY IN MEMORY. `mapinfo` proves it is a real
;     level with real monsters; no file is ever written, and `savemap` must not
;     invent one.
;   - the AREA decides. A moor (undead, difficulty 0.45) and a road (0.05) are
;     asked separately, and the sizes differ because difficulty scales them.
;   - saving inside one is REFUSED rather than written, which is its own
;     script (worldnosave.eval) because the runner counts a refused command as
;     a script failure — and rightly: asking for the impossible is a fault
;     everywhere except where it is the measurement.
;   - leaving puts the party back on the world map WHERE IT STOOD, not at a
;     doorway: it came from open ground.
;   - and a save made there, out of the ambush, is written and loads (below).
logecho on
reset
seed 4
leave

; --- on the road: a small, mild encounter ------------------------------------
worldpos 6 6
encounter
state
mapinfo
monsters

; --- back out to exactly where we were ---------------------------------------
leave
world

; --- on the moor: harder ground, a bigger space ------------------------------
worldpos 5 3
encounter
mapinfo
monsters
leave
world

; --- a save on the road after it (code-review C299) --------------------------
; Out of the ambush, it is still the level loaded under the world map (nothing
; replaces it until a doorway does) - and every save was refused until the
; party went underground. The save is allowed now, and names the level PARKED
; before the ambushes (the harness ground, left by the first `leave`), not the
; ambush, which has no file: the load opens that level, parks it again, and
; lands on the world map where the party stood.
echo --- encounter: saved on the road ---
save encounterleft
load encounterleft
echo --- encounter: loaded ---
state
world
mapinfo
discover crypt_gate
enter crypt_gate
mapinfo
